## Frequently Asked Questions

### 1. Does CBT really work for anxiety?

CBT is an established, evidence-based psychological treatment for several anxiety disorders. Research supports its use, although individual results vary depending on the condition, treatment approach, and personal circumstances.

### 2. What are the most common CBT techniques for anxiety?

Common techniques include cognitive restructuring, exposure therapy, behavioral experiments, self-monitoring, and problem-solving strategies. A therapist selects techniques based on the person's symptoms and treatment goals.

### 3. Can CBT help with overthinking and excessive worrying?

CBT may help people recognize repetitive worry patterns, examine assumptions, develop more balanced thinking, and change behaviors that reinforce anxiety. It can be particularly relevant when worry is persistent and difficult to control.

### 4. Is CBT suitable for social anxiety?

Yes. CBT is commonly used to treat social anxiety. Treatment may include examining fears of judgment, changing avoidance patterns, practising behavioral experiments, and gradually approaching social situations with appropriate support.

### 5. How many CBT sessions are needed for anxiety?

The number of sessions varies. It depends on the type and severity of anxiety, treatment goals, personal circumstances, and progress. A therapist can discuss an appropriate treatment plan after an assessment.

### 6. Can CBT be done online?

Yes. CBT can be delivered through in-person or virtual therapy, depending on the provider and the person's needs. The NIH notes that psychotherapy, including CBT, can be effective when delivered in person or virtually.

### 7. What should I expect during my first CBT session?

Your therapist will generally ask about your concerns, symptoms, relevant experiences, and goals. You can discuss what you hope to change, ask questions about CBT, and explore whether the approach feels appropriate.

### 8. Does Riaz Counseling offer CBT for anxiety?

Riaz Counseling lists CBT among the approaches used in its individual therapy services and identifies anxiety as one of the concerns it supports. You can contact the practice to ask which therapists currently offer CBT and whether their experience matches your needs.

## Conclusion

Anxiety can influence how you think, feel, behave, and approach everyday situations. When worry and avoidance begin limiting your choices, understanding the patterns behind them can be an important step toward change.

Cognitive behavioral therapy offers a structured way to recognize unhelpful thoughts, examine anxious predictions, develop practical coping strategies, and gradually approach situations that have become difficult.

You do not have to manage every concern alone or wait until anxiety becomes unbearable before seeking support. If you are considering CBT for anxiety, speaking with a qualified mental health professional can help you understand your options and decide what approach may be right for you.

Riaz Counseling offers individual therapy for anxiety and lists CBT among its therapeutic approaches. Learning more about the available services and speaking with a therapist can be a practical place to begin.

### Mental Health Disclaimer

This article is for general educational purposes and is not a substitute for professional mental health or medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Anxiety symptoms can have different causes, and a qualified professional can help determine what may be contributing to your experience.

If you are in immediate danger or experiencing a mental health crisis in the United States, call 911 for an emergency or call or text 988 for the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline.

